Z||000000||eagle01||12-03-1999||10:39 PM||||hi everyone<P>this is my first time to post a question. i just put in an 86 v-6 motor in my 85 fiero and when i turn on the ignition, it starts up and runs for about 3 seconds but then it dies immediately. just my guess but sounds like a fuel starvation problem?  this is a cry for help.  thanks in advance for your<BR>responses.<P>ed here in southern california||||reg||1
Z||000001||Phaeton||12-03-1999||11:43 PM||||Not knowing what has been tried already I would start at the oil press switch and harness assembly as this controls the power to the fuel pump.  The weatherseal on the harness has a tendency to leak and the moisture corrodes the contacts in the fuel pump switch which is oil pressure activated.||||reg||6
Z||000002||Phaeton||12-04-1999||12:14 AM||||The oil pressure switch controls the power to the fuel pump and the weatherseal on the harness is prone to letting moisture through, then the connectors get corrosion and give bad oil press readings, leave the fuel pump on 7/24, don't turn the pump on at all or any combination of the above.  This is just the easiest thing it could be, assuming the fuel pump worked flawlessly before the swap.||||reg||5
Z||000003||AL||12-04-1999||01:40 AM||||HEY EAGLE, WHERE IN CALIF, I'M FROM BLOOMINGTON NEXT TO FONTANA.||||reg||1
Z||000004||fiero56||12-04-1999||08:33 AM||||If you replaced a 4-cyl with a v-6, you also must change over to the v-6 fuel pump(Was it a 4-cyl engine before?). Replace your fuel filter too, it may be clogged. You should be able to hear the fuel pump run for about 2 seconds if you turn the key to the run position with the engine off.  If not, maybe a relay or the pump itself.  sounds to me like the pump builds enough pressure with the engine off, but once fuel is needed(i.e. engine running) the pump cannot supply enough pressure and therefore the engine dies.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 12-04-1999).]||||reg||1
